#!/usr/bin/env bash
# orphan-ok: on-demand delegation/attenuation primitive — pulled when a caller mints/derives/
#   verifies; not a scheduled sense or reflex. It is deliberately at ZERO adoption and does NOT
#   gate mesh-organ — see THE DECISION below, which is the reason, not an omission.
# mesh-cap — DERIVABLE CAPABILITY TOKENS (study 'microkernel' / LiberSystem / seL4, idea-queue
# 2026-07-17T04:23:01Z). Written as the richer counterpart to mesh-organ's `# cap-allow:` header
# (2026-07-18 STUDY 'capability security'), which is a STATIC allow-list — a caller is either in the
# list or not. It is NOT that header's replacement and does not gate it (THE DECISION, below). seL4/L4/CapROS/LiberSystem have a richer primitive: a cap is a TOKEN you can DERIVE,
# minting a strictly weaker child cap from a parent (the one-way subset ratchet), and the kernel is
# the only minting authority (so tokens are unforgeable). This tool brings that to bash.
#
# THE MODEL:
#   - A CAPABILITY = a signed token carrying {grantor, rights[], depth, optional for-target}.
#   - UNFORGEABILITY: the token is HMAC-SHA256'd against a per-node secret (~/.mesh/cap-secret,
#     0600, generated from /dev/urandom on first use). The secret is the "kernel" — without it you
#     cannot mint, alter, or forge a token. To forge a cap you would need to read the 0600 secret
#     file, which is the strongest unforgeability analog available in userspace bash. We are honest
#     about the gap: a process with the secret's read-permission CAN forge (root, or uid-owner);
#     seL4 closes this at the kernel boundary, we close it at the file boundary.
#   - SUBSET RATCHET (the seL4 'mint' rule): derive(parent, R) requires R ⊆ parent.R — derived
#     rights must be a subset. You can drop rights, you can NEVER add them. Refused (exit 3) otherwise.
#   - DEPTH RATCHET: each token has a depth counter (root default 8); derive decrements; refuses
#     at 0. Prevents infinite cap-merchant chains (a real CapROS concern — delegated authority
#     shouldn't propagate forever without re-authorization).
#   - LINEAGE: each token carries parent-hash (ROOT for minted) + grantor + issued-at, so a chain
#     is auditable post-hoc. inspect() prints it.
#   - SCOPE: per-NODE only (each node = own kernel/secret). A token minted on mesh-home is INVALID
#     on imozerov — deliberate. Cross-node caps are a different design (federated minting).
#
# THE DECISION — THIS DOES NOT GATE mesh-organ, AND WILL NOT (2026-08-20, board task
# 'mesh-cap-has-no-consumers-so-its-guarantees-are-untested-by-use'). This tool was written as the
# richer half of mesh-organ's static `# cap-allow:` header, so the obvious next step reads as
# "verify an unforgeable token at cap_gate instead of trusting a self-asserted name". That step was
# MEASURED instead of assumed, and the measurement kills it. mesh-organ's caller identity is
# self-asserted (MESH_CALLER / --as / the tmux window name), so the only adversary a token gate
# there would exclude is a caller that LIES ABOUT ITS NAME. Every mesh caller runs as the SAME uid
# (1000) that owns ~/.mesh/cap-secret — so the very shell that can `export MESH_CALLER=genome` can
# equally read the 0600 secret and mint itself `--rights=read,write,admin --for=<anything>`. Both
# were run live on mesh-home in ONE ordinary shell, no privilege of any kind, and both worked. A
# token gate at mesh-organ therefore excludes NOBODY it does not already admit, while adding a
# fail-closed DENY path in front of live actuators (camera, SMS, TTS) whose rollback would have to
# run through the organ router it just gated. Availability cost, zero security gain: the answer is
# no. Note this is the same boundary the unforgeability bullet above already declares — the claim
# was always uid-scoped; what is new is naming the consumer that scoping disqualifies.
#   WHAT WOULD CHANGE THE ANSWER: a caller that does NOT share the secret's uid — a sandboxed
#   subagent, a separate service account, a token crossing a real privilege boundary. Wire it then,
#   with the rollback living outside mesh-organ. Until such a caller exists, adding one here would
#   be a gate whose only measurable effect is a new way for the camera to refuse to fire.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR, THEN: an AUDITED ATTENUATION primitive, not an authentication barrier. The
# guarantees that survive the same-uid threat model are the BOOKKEEPING ones, and they are the
# useful half: a delegating caller hands a strictly weaker cap onward (subset ratchet), bound to
# one named target (target ratchet), depth-limited, with an auditable parent chain. That constrains
# the HONEST delegate — which is what a delegation chain between cooperating minds actually needs —
# and it makes an over-broad hand-off a REFUSAL at the point of delegation instead of a discovery
# afterwards. Zero callers today is a stated position, not a gap; and a green --test asserts the
# tool, never its use, so nothing here should be read as an integration that happened.
#
# USAGE (a delegation chain — no mesh-organ involvement):
#   token="$(mesh-cap mint --rights=genome,read --for=camera-cap)"
#   mesh-cap verify "$token" --action=read --for=camera-cap && echo ALLOWED || echo DENIED
#   child="$(mesh-cap derive "$token" --rights=read)"   # drop 'genome' right, keep 'read'
#   NAME THE TARGET. A cap minted --for=X refuses a verify that does not say which target it is
#   acting on (exit 3) — the earlier form here omitted it and thereby demonstrated the fail-open
#   call it was meant to prevent. And ONLY exit 0 is ALLOW: rc 2 (no secret / n/a) is NOT a pass,
#   so consumers gate with `&& allow`, never with `|| deny`-style inversions of a non-zero rc.
#
# EXIT CODES (the mesh's honest-unavailable doctrine):
#   0  ok (mint emitted / verify passed / inspect printed)
#   1  usage / smoke-FAIL
#   2  n/a (no secret, organ absent — caller's invocation gate passes through)
#   3  DENY (forged / escalated / depth-exhausted / wrong-action — the cap-system refusal)
#
#   mesh-cap mint --rights=<r,...> [--for=<target>] [--max-depth=N] [--as=<grantor>]
#   mesh-cap derive <parent> --rights=<r,...> [--for=<target>] [--as=<grantor>]
#   mesh-cap verify <token> --action=<r>        # exit 0 ok / 3 denied / 2 n/a
#   mesh-cap inspect <token>                    # decoded metadata, NO auth check (read-only)
#   mesh-cap --test
#
# STDLIB ONLY. System python3 has no numpy here — and this tool uses none (json/hmac/hashlib/base64/
# os/urandom are all stdlib). The signing primitive is deliberately in python3 not bash because bash
# has no native HMAC; the bash half is arg-parse + file mgmt + exit-code mapping (the unix shell's
# actual job).
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"

MESH="${MESH_DIR:-$HOME/.mesh}"
SECRET="$MESH/cap-secret"
[ -d "$MESH" ] || mkdir -p "$MESH"

# ensure_secret: idempotent — generate 32 random bytes (256-bit) from /dev/urandom if absent.
# 0600 so only the uid-owner can read it (the unforgeability boundary). NEVER overwrite an existing
# one: losing the secret invalidates every outstanding token (loud break, not silent).
ensure_secret(){
  [ -f "$SECRET" ] && return 0
  head -c 32 /dev/urandom > "$SECRET" || { echo "mesh-cap: cannot write $SECRET (no /dev/urandom?)" >&2; exit 2; }
  chmod 600 "$SECRET" || true
}

# pyeval: run the cap-engine python (read from heredoc) with the secret + args on argv.
# Stdin = the engine script. Argv: $1=mode, then mode-specific args. The secret is passed via
# env var (MESH_CAP_SECRET) so it never appears in ps/cmdline output (process-list leakage).
pyeval(){ MESH_CAP_SECRET="$(cat "$SECRET" 2>/dev/null | base64)" python3 - "$@" <<'PYEOF'; }
import sys, os, json, hmac, hashlib, base64, time
SECRET = base64.b64decode(os.environ.get("MESH_CAP_SECRET","")) if os.environ.get("MESH_CAP_SECRET") else b""
def die(msg, rc): sys.stderr.write(f"mesh-cap: {msg}\n"); sys.exit(rc)
def sign(payload_b64):
    mac = hmac.new(SECRET, payload_b64.encode(), hashlib.sha256).digest()
    return base64.urlsafe_b64encode(mac).decode().rstrip("=")
def verify_sig(payload_b64, sig_b64):
    expect = sign(payload_b64)
    return hmac.compare_digest(expect, sig_b64)
def emit(tok):
    payload = json.dumps(tok, sort_keys=True, separators=(",", ":")).encode()
    p = base64.urlsafe_b64encode(payload).decode().rstrip("=")
    return f"{p}.{sign(p)}"
def parse(tok):
    if not tok or "." not in tok: die("malformed token (no '.')", 3)
    p, s = tok.rsplit(".", 1)
    if not verify_sig(p, s): die("FORGED — signature mismatch (token altered, or minted under a different node-secret)", 3)
    try: pad = p + "=" * (-len(p) % 4); return json.loads(base64.urlsafe_b64decode(pad))
    except Exception as e: die(f"malformed payload: {e}", 3)
def H(tok): return hashlib.sha256(tok.encode()).hexdigest()[:12]   # short lineage hash

mode = sys.argv[1] if len(sys.argv) > 1 else ""
if not SECRET and mode not in ("--help","-h",""):
    # n/a path: no secret + not the help arm. Caller's gate (exit 2 = honest n/a) passes through.
    die("no secret (organ-absent — call `mesh-cap mint` once to generate one)", 2)

if mode in ("--help","-h",""):
    print("usage: mesh-cap mint|derive|verify|inspect|--test  (see header for full contract)"); sys.exit(0)

if mode == "inspect":
    tok = sys.argv[2] if len(sys.argv) > 2 else ""
    t = parse(tok)   # verifies sig — forged → exit 3 here
    print(f"grantor : {t.get('g','?')}")
    print(f"rights  : {','.join(t.get('r',[]))}")
    print(f"depth   : {t.get('d','?')}")
    print(f"for     : {t.get('f','-')}")
    print(f"parent  : {t.get('p','?')}")
    print(f"thash   : {H(tok)}")
    print(f"issued  : {time.strftime('%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ', time.gmtime(t.get('t',0)))}")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode == "mint":
    rights, for_, depth, grantor = set(), None, 8, None
    a = sys.argv[2:]
    i = 0
    while i < len(a):
        if a[i].startswith("--rights="): rights = {r.strip() for r in a[i][9:].split(",") if r.strip()}
        elif a[i].startswith("--for="): for_ = a[i][6:]
        elif a[i].startswith("--max-depth="): depth = int(a[i][12:])
        elif a[i].startswith("--as="): grantor = a[i][5:]
        else: die(f"mint: unknown arg '{a[i]}'", 1)
        i += 1
    if not rights: die("mint: --rights=<r,...> required", 1)
    grantor = grantor or os.environ.get("MESH_CALLER") or "unknown"
    tok = {"v":1, "g":grantor, "r":sorted(rights), "d":depth, "f":for_, "t":int(time.time()), "p":"ROOT"}
    print(emit(tok)); sys.exit(0)

if mode == "derive":
    if len(sys.argv) < 3: die("derive: <parent-token> required", 1)
    parent_tok = sys.argv[2]
    pt = parse(parent_tok)   # forged → exit 3
    rights, for_, grantor = set(), None, None
    a = sys.argv[3:]
    i = 0
    while i < len(a):
        if a[i].startswith("--rights="): rights = {r.strip() for r in a[i][9:].split(",") if r.strip()}
        elif a[i].startswith("--for="): for_ = a[i][6:]
        elif a[i].startswith("--as="): grantor = a[i][5:]
        else: die(f"derive: unknown arg '{a[i]}'", 1)
        i += 1
    if not rights: die("derive: --rights=<r,...> required (subset of parent)", 1)
    parent_rights = set(pt.get("r", []))
    # SUBSET RATCHET (seL4 mint rule): derived rights MUST be ⊆ parent rights.
    if not rights.issubset(parent_rights):
        extra = rights - parent_rights
        die(f"REFUSED — escalation: requested rights {{{','.join(sorted(extra))}}} not in parent {{{','.join(sorted(parent_rights))}}} (cap derivation is a one-way ratchet, seL4 mint rule)", 3)
    # DEPTH RATCHET: a depth-0 cap cannot propagate further (re-authorization required).
    if pt.get("d", 0) <= 0:
        die(f"REFUSED — depth-exhausted (parent depth=0; delegate from a fresh root or raise --max-depth at mint)", 3)
    # TARGET RATCHET (the same seL4 mint rule, applied to the FOR-BINDING — it was enforced on
    # rights only, so `f` was freely re-pointable: a cap bound to camera-cap derived cleanly into
    # one bound to sms-cap, keeping a strict rights subset the whole way. Narrowing an UNBOUND
    # parent to a target is a narrowing and stays legal; re-pointing a BOUND parent is escalation.)
    parent_for = pt.get("f")
    if for_ is not None and parent_for is not None and for_ != parent_for:
        die(f"REFUSED — target escalation: parent cap is bound to '{parent_for}', child asked for '{for_}' (a bound cap narrows to its own target or inherits it; it never re-points)", 3)
    grantor = grantor or os.environ.get("MESH_CALLER") or pt.get("g", "unknown")
    tok = {"v":1, "g":grantor, "r":sorted(rights), "d":pt["d"]-1, "f":for_ or parent_for,
           "t":int(time.time()), "p":H(parent_tok)}
    print(emit(tok)); sys.exit(0)

if mode == "verify":
    if len(sys.argv) < 3: die("verify: <token> required", 1)
    tok = sys.argv[2]
    action, target, target_named = None, None, False
    a = sys.argv[3:]
    i = 0
    while i < len(a):
        if a[i].startswith("--action="): action = a[i][9:].strip()
        elif a[i].startswith("--for="): target, target_named = a[i][6:], True
        else: die(f"verify: unknown arg '{a[i]}'", 1)
        i += 1
    if not action: die("verify: --action=<r> required", 1)
    t = parse(tok)   # forged → exit 3
    bound = t.get("f")
    # TARGET BINDING IS FAIL-CLOSED, and this is the whole point of the field. It used to be checked
    # only when the CHECKER opted in by passing --for=, which puts the narrowing on the wrong side:
    # a cap minted --for=camera-cap authorised `--action=read` against anything at all, and the
    # tool's own documented CONSUMER PATTERN made exactly that call. A binding a caller can decline
    # to check is not a binding — so a BOUND cap now refuses any verify that does not name a target.
    if bound:
        if not target_named:
            die(f"DENY — cap is BOUND to target '{bound}' and this verify named none; pass --for=<target> (a binding the checker may skip is not a binding)", 3)
        if bound != target:
            die(f"DENY — for-target mismatch (cap bound to '{bound}', asked for '{target}')", 3)
    # An UNBOUND cap (minted with no --for) is universal by construction: it authorises its rights
    # against any target, so naming one here is accepted and simply carries no extra constraint.
    rights = set(t.get("r", []))
    if action in rights: sys.exit(0)
    die(f"DENY — action '{action}' not in cap rights {{{','.join(sorted(rights))}}}", 3)

die(f"unknown mode '{mode}' (try: mint, derive, verify, inspect, --test)", 1)
PYEOF

# ─────────── --test (RED-first; mutation-verified; temp HOME so it writes no real artifacts) ─────
[ "${1:-}" = "--test" ] && {
  command -v python3 >/dev/null 2>&1 || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no python3)"; exit 1; }
  tmp="$(mktemp -d)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mktemp)"; exit 1; }
  trap 'rm -rf "$tmp"' EXIT
  mkdir -p "$tmp/.mesh"
  echo "=== T1: mint emits a parseable signed token ==="
  tok="$(HOME="$tmp" MESH_CALLER=genome "$0" mint --rights=genome,read,write --for=camera-cap)"
  [ -n "$tok" ] && [ "$(printf '%s' "$tok" | tr -cd '.' | wc -c)" = 1 ] \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mint did not emit a '<payload>.<sig>' token)"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T2: verify ok for action IN rights ==="
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$tok" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (verify read should be exit 0)"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T3: verify DENY for action NOT in rights ==="
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$tok" --action=admin --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (verify admin should DENY exit 3, got rc=$rc)"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T4: derive with SUBSET rights succeeds (the seL4 mint rule) ==="
  child="$(HOME="$tmp" MESH_CALLER=tg "$0" derive "$tok" --rights=read 2>/dev/null)" \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive subset {read} from {genome,read,write} should succeed)"; exit 1; }
  [ -n "$child" ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive emitted empty token)"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T5: derived child has the SUBSET only (not parent's full rights) ==="
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$child" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (child should still verify read)"; exit 1; }
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$child" --action=write --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (child verify write should DENY — 'write' was dropped at derive); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T6: derive with NON-subset rights is REFUSED (escalation gate) ==="
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$tok" --rights=admin 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive {admin} from {genome,read,write} should REFUSE exit 3 — escalation); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T7: FORGED token (tampered payload, stale sig) is REJECTED ==="
  # Tamper: flip one char in the payload half, keep the sig half.
  tampered="$(printf '%s' "$tok" | sed 's/^./X/')"
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$tampered" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (tampered payload should be rejected as FORGED exit 3); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T8: token minted under a DIFFERENT secret (different 'kernel') is REJECTED ==="
  tmp2="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$tmp2/.mesh"
  HOME="$tmp2" "$0" mint --rights=read >/dev/null 2>&1   # generate tmp2's own secret
  HOME="$tmp2" "$0" verify "$tok" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (token from a different node-secret should be FORGED exit 3 — per-node scope); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }
  rm -rf "$tmp2"

  echo "=== T9: depth ratchet — derive exhausts, refuses at 0 ==="
  shallow="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" mint --rights=r --max-depth=1)"
  d1="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$shallow" --rights=r 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (first derive from depth=1 should succeed)"; exit 1; }
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$d1" --rights=r 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive from depth-0 token should REFUSE exit 3 — depth-exhausted); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T10: for-target binding — cap verifies for its bound target, DENY for another ==="
  bound="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" mint --rights=read --for=camera-cap)"
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$bound" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cap should verify for its bound --for target)"; exit 1; }
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$bound" --action=read --for=mic-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cap should DENY for a different --for target); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T14: a BOUND cap REFUSES a verify that names no target (fail-CLOSED binding) ==="
  # The pre-fix hole, measured live 2026-08-20: `verify $bound --action=read` returned 0, so a cap
  # minted --for=camera-cap authorised a read against anything. The check existed but fired only
  # when the CHECKER passed --for=, i.e. the narrowing was opt-in on the wrong side.
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$bound" --action=read 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (a cap BOUND to camera-cap verified with NO --for target — fail-open binding); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }
  # THE rc ABOVE IS NOT THE DISCRIMINATING ASSERTION, and saying so is cheaper than a leg nobody can
  # falsify: with the mismatch check present, an absent target compares None against the binding and
  # yields 3 anyway, so the only mutant that reddens this rc kills the whole block — and T10 catches
  # that one first. Pre-fix the code was fail-OPEN precisely because the comparison lived INSIDE the
  # `--for=` arm and therefore never ran when no target was named. What separates "closed by design"
  # from "closed by an accident of a None comparison" is WHICH refusal comes back, so the message
  # below is the real gate; it is the leg that reddens when the missing-target branch is removed.
  # capture, THEN grep: the DENY's own exit 3 is the left side of a pipe under `set -o pipefail`,
  # so piping it straight into grep makes the pipeline return 3 and fires the failure branch even
  # when grep matched (seen RED exactly that way while writing this gate)
  _dmsg="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$bound" --action=read 2>&1)"
  printf '%s' "$_dmsg" | grep -q "BOUND to target 'camera-cap'" \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the refusal does not name the binding it enforced — a DENY that cannot be acted on): $_dmsg"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T15: TARGET RATCHET — a bound cap cannot be DERIVED onto a different target ==="
  # Measured live pre-fix: derive $camera_cap --rights=read --for=sms-cap succeeded and the child
  # verified against sms-cap. Rights ratcheted down the whole way; the seL4 mint rule was simply
  # never applied to the for-binding, so authority moved SIDEWAYS instead of narrowing.
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$bound" --rights=read --for=sms-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive RE-POINTED a camera-cap to sms-cap — the target ratchet is absent); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }
  # …and it must not over-refuse: keeping the SAME target, and inheriting by omission, both stay legal
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$bound" --rights=read --for=camera-cap >/dev/null 2>&1 \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive to the SAME target was refused — a ratchet that refuses everything is a stoppage, not a ratchet)"; exit 1; }
  inh="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$bound" --rights=read 2>/dev/null)" \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (derive inheriting the parent binding was refused)"; exit 1; }
  _ins="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" inspect "$inh" 2>/dev/null)"
  printf '%s' "$_ins" | grep -q '^for     : camera-cap$' \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (child did not INHERIT the parent's binding — an unbound child would be a silent widening)"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T16: narrowing an UNBOUND parent to a target is still allowed; unbound verify unchanged ==="
  free="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" mint --rights=read)"
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$free" --action=read 2>/dev/null \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (an UNBOUND cap must still verify with no target — the fix must not break the universal case)"; exit 1; }
  nar="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" derive "$free" --rights=read --for=tv-cap 2>/dev/null)" \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (binding an unbound parent to a target is a NARROWING and must be allowed)"; exit 1; }
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$nar" --action=read --for=tv-cap 2>/dev/null \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the narrowed child should verify for its own target)"; exit 1; }
  HOME="$tmp" "$0" verify "$nar" --action=read --for=camera-cap 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 3 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (the narrowed child must DENY another target); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T11: lineage — inspect shows parent chain (ROOT for minted, hash for derived) ==="
  ins="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" inspect "$child" 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (inspect should succeed on valid child token)"; exit 1; }
  printf '%s' "$ins" | grep -q '^parent  : [0-9a-f]\{12\}$' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (child's parent should be a 12-hex hash of the parent token); got: $(printf '%s' "$ins" | grep ^parent)"; exit 1; }
  insroot="$(HOME="$tmp" "$0" inspect "$tok" 2>/dev/null)"
  printf '%s' "$insroot" | grep -q '^parent  : ROOT$' || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (minted token's parent should be 'ROOT')"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T12: secret file is 0600 (the unforgeability boundary) ==="
  [ "$(stat -c '%a' "$tmp/.mesh/cap-secret" 2>/dev/null)" = "600" ] \
    || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (cap-secret should be 0600 so only the uid-owner can read it; got $(stat -c '%a' "$tmp/.mesh/cap-secret" 2>/dev/null))"; exit 1; }

  echo "=== T13: honest n/a — no secret + verify = exit 2 (caller's gate passes through) ==="
  tmp3="$(mktemp -d)"; mkdir -p "$tmp3/.mesh"
  HOME="$tmp3" "$0" verify "fake.token" --action=read 2>/dev/null; rc=$?
  [ "$rc" = 2 ] || { echo "smoke-test: FAIL (no-secret verify should exit 2 honest-n/a — the organ-absent doctrine); got rc=$rc"; exit 1; }
  rm -rf "$tmp3"

  echo "=== T17: THE DECISION tripwire — mesh-organ must NOT call this tool ==="
  # A decision recorded only in a comment is a channel with no reader: the next mind to see
  # "capability tokens" beside "static allow-list" will wire them together without reaching THE
  # DECISION block above. So the decision is asserted here as a FACT ABOUT ANOTHER FILE, and this
  # gate goes RED the moment it stops holding — pointing at the block instead of at a diff. Seen
  # red by planting `mesh-cap verify` into a copy of mesh-organ. No self-match risk: the pattern
  # searches mesh-organ, never $0. If mesh-organ is absent this is a LOUD skip, never a silent pass.
  _organ=""
  for _c in "$(dirname "$0")/mesh-organ" "$HOME/lte-workstation/scripts/mesh-organ" "$(command -v mesh-organ 2>/dev/null)"; do
    [ -n "$_c" ] && [ -f "$_c" ] && { _organ="$_c"; break; }
  done
  if [ -z "$_organ" ]; then
    echo "  T17 SKIPPED — mesh-organ not found on this node (the tripwire has no subject here)"
  else
    if grep -nE '(^|[^-[:alnum:]])mesh-cap[[:space:]]+(mint|derive|verify|inspect)' "$_organ" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
      echo "smoke-test: FAIL (mesh-organ now CALLS mesh-cap — that integration was decided AGAINST on"
      echo "  2026-08-20 and the reason is measured, not stylistic: mesh-organ's caller identity is"
      echo "  self-asserted and every mesh caller shares the uid that owns ~/.mesh/cap-secret, so a"
      echo "  token gate there excludes nobody it does not already admit while adding a fail-closed"
      echo "  DENY path in front of camera/SMS/TTS. Read THE DECISION in this file's header; if a"
      echo "  cross-uid caller now exists, change the decision AND this gate together, deliberately."
      grep -nE '(^|[^-[:alnum:]])mesh-cap[[:space:]]+(mint|derive|verify|inspect)' "$_organ" | sed 's/^/    /'
      exit 1
    fi
    echo "  T17 ok — $_organ calls no mesh-cap verb (decision holds)"
  fi

  echo "smoke-test: ok (17 gates: mint/verify/deny/subset/escalation/forged/cross-secret/depth/target-binding + FAIL-CLOSED binding [a bound cap refuses a verify naming no target, and the DENY names the binding] + TARGET RATCHET [a bound cap cannot be derived onto another target, same-target and inherit-by-omission still legal, child inherits the binding] + unbound stays universal and narrows cleanly/lineage/perm/honest-na + THE DECISION tripwire [mesh-organ calls no cap verb])"
  exit 0
}

# ─────────── dispatch (only when NOT --test) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
case "${1:-}" in
  mint|derive)
    # WRITE op: auto-create the per-node secret if absent (idempotent — never overwrites).
    ensure_secret
    pyeval "$@"
    exit $? ;;
  verify|inspect)
    # READ op: do NOT auto-create. If no secret exists, the cap system is organ-absent on this node
    # → honest exit 2 (caller's invocation gate passes through). A read op that mints the secret as
    # a side-effect would be the silent-fallback doctrine's exact trap (a forged token looks "real"
    # against a fresh secret). The secret is the kernel-authority; you don't mint a kernel by asking.
    [ -f "$SECRET" ] || { echo "mesh-cap: no secret ($SECRET) — cap system organ-absent on this node (run 'mesh-cap mint' to initialize)" >&2; exit 2; }
    pyeval "$@"
    exit $? ;;
  "") echo "usage: mesh-cap mint|derive|verify|inspect|--test  (see header)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
  *)  echo "mesh-cap: unknown mode '${1:-}' (try: mint, derive, verify, inspect, --test)" >&2; exit 1 ;;
esac
